  "statement": "I concede that the current safety discourse risks becoming a tool for regulatory capture if it remains an abstract, industry-led exercise, and I agree with the critique that protecting incumbent oligopolies under the guise of safety is a fundamental danger to the public interest. When safety standards are written by the same firms they are meant to restrain, they inevitably function as a barrier to entry rather than a mechanism for public protection. To prevent this, we must move beyond the boardroom and ground our governance in the physical reality of AI production, starting with the tangible externalities of the infrastructure itself.\n\nTreating large-scale data centers as high-impact environmental hazards is not a distraction from safety; it is the necessary first layer of a robust precautionary regime. By mandating strict, transparent oversight of the massive water, energy, and land resources required for these systems, we establish a baseline of industrial accountability that is far harder to manipulate than vague, self-reported 'alignment' benchmarks. This approach forces developers to internalize the physical costs of their operations, effectively shifting the burden of proof from the public to the provider before they are permitted to scale further.\n\nInstitutional accountability must be democratized by linking operational licenses to measurable physical impacts, ensuring that the providers themselves bear the financial and legal consequences of their footprint. This creates a 'skin-in-the-game' model where the financial stakes of deployment are tied to real-world performance and ecological impact, rather than just market-share growth. By establishing this foundation of tangible oversight, we create a verifiable, democratic framework that serves as a prerequisite for managing more complex, emerging hazards, effectively neutralizing the risk of regulatory capture while ensuring that infrastructure development remains subordinate to the public good.",
